= Solution
{parent=iii}

**The first implication is false.** On the <Noetherian scheme> $\operatorname{Spec}k[t]$, the <short exact sequence of sheaves>
$$
0\longrightarrow\mathcal O_X\xrightarrow{\,t\,}\mathcal O_X\longrightarrow\widetilde{k[t]/(t)}\longrightarrow0
$$
has locally free first and middle terms. The quotient is not <locally free>: at $(t)$ it is a nonzero module annihilated by $t$, whereas a free module over the <integral domain> $k[t]_{(t)}$ has no such element.

**The second implication is true.** Near any point trivialize $\mathcal F$ and $\mathcal H$. Choose a finite local basis of $\mathcal H$. Surjectivity as a sheaf gives local lifts of its basis sections; shrink to the intersection of their finitely many neighbourhoods. The lifts define a splitting $\mathcal H\to\mathcal G$ on that neighbourhood. Thus $\mathcal G\cong\mathcal F\oplus\mathcal H$ there and is <locally free>. This is <local splitting when a quotient sheaf is locally free>; no global splitting is asserted.

